## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a privacy-preserving multi-tenant system with proper budget accounting, policy binding, and audit capabilities. It assesses understanding of differential privacy concepts (epsilon, delta), tenant isolation, and API design for secure batch processing.

## Key Concepts
- **Multi-tenant privacy budgets:** Each tenant controls their own epsilon/delta budget per batch, ensuring aggregate privacy guarantees
- **Data scope and policy binding:** Policy_id associates a batch with specific data handling rules; data_scope defines which data fields are included
- **Budget accounting:** Track cumulative privacy spend to prevent over-disclosure
- **Ledger/audit trail:** Immutable logging of all operations enables compliance verification and replay protection
- **Signature verification:** Ensures request integrity and non-repudiation across tenant boundaries

## Code Example
```javascript
// Data model example
type BatchSubmission = {
  tenant_id: string;
  batch_id: string;
  model_version: string;
  client_timestamp: string;
  privacy_budget: { epsilon: number; delta: number };
  policy_id: string;
  data_scope: string;
  signature: string;
  score_shares: string[];
};

// Budget enforcement middleware
async function enforceBudget(submission: BatchSubmission) {
  const spent = await getCumulativeSpend(submission.tenant_id);
  if (spent.epsilon + submission.privacy_budget.epsilon > MAX_EPSILON) {
    throw new Error('Privacy budget exceeded');
  }
}

// Tenant isolation check
function isolateTenant(tenantId: string, batchId: string) {
  return db.batch.find({ tenant_id: tenantId, batch_id: batchId });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests designing a real-time privacy-preserving federation with streaming updates, focusing on DP budgets, monotonic aggregation, replay protection, and fault tolerance.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time streaming aggregation with privacy guarantees
- Per-device DP budgeting and accounting
- Replay protection and clock skew handling
- Throughput considerations and backpressure in edge-to-cloud pipelines

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: validate delta
function processDelta({device_id, ts, delta, nonce, dp_noise}) {
  if (nonce in usedNonces[device_id]) throw 'replay';
  usedNonces[device_id].add(nonce);
  if (ts <= lastTs[device_id]) delta = 0; // monotone
  // apply clipping
  delta = Math.max(-MAX_DELTA, Math.min(MAX_DELTA, delta));
  globalSum += delta + dp_noise;
  budgets[device_id] -= DP_COST;
}
```

